{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"ce8bf760-041e-439f-9489-0dac7b0c5049","title":"Definite integral as the area under a curve"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ce8bf760-041e-439f-9489-0dac7b0c5049","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A definite integral measures signed area between a curve and the horizontal axis. Let’s begin with a curve that stays above the axis.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Definite integral = signed area"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[0,2.3],"y":[0,5],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"curve","y":"x^2","domain":[0,2.15],"label":"y = x²"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"integral","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\int_0^2 x^2\\,dx"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ce8bf760-041e-439f-9489-0dac7b0c5049","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The limits zero and two select this region. Because the curve is above the axis, its integral equals the region’s ordinary area.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"region","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"shade","under":"curve","from":0,"to":2},{"kind":"point","id":"endpoint","at":{"on":"curve","x":2},"label":"(2, 4)"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"endpoint"},"to":[2,0],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.45,0.8],"text":"area"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"areaidentity","anchor":{"el":"integral","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A=\\int_0^2 x^2\\,dx"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ce8bf760-041e-439f-9489-0dac7b0c5049","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Imagine filling the region with narrow rectangles. Each contributes height times width; adding them and shrinking their widths gives the integral.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"slices","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","points":[[1.25,0],[1.5,0],[1.5,1.5625],[1.25,1.5625]],"closed":true,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"measure","from":[1.25,0.22],"to":[1.5,0.22],"label":"Δx"},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"curve","x":1.25}}]},{"op":"equation","id":"sum","anchor":{"el":"areaidentity","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\sum_i f(x_i)\\,\\Delta x\\ \\longrightarrow\\ \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ce8bf760-041e-439f-9489-0dac7b0c5049","step":{"id":"s4","script":"An antiderivative computes the exact area: evaluate at the upper limit, then subtract the lower-limit value. Here the area is eight thirds.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"sum","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^2=\\frac{8}{3}"},{"op":"figure","id":"value","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1.52,0.48],"text":"8/3"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the curve lies below the horizontal axis, what sign does that region contribute to the integral?","expected_gist":"Negative; the integral measures signed area."}}}
